lated steering lock and passed through the inverse of its input shaping; an aim behind the car takes full lock toward it. The chase index, reach radius, re-anchor and route-bound progress are unchanged. Wall feelers (`racing_line::sense_walls`): each frame four horizontal rays from 0.5 m above the body origin — ±0.3 rad reaching 6 m + 0.6 s of travel (≤30 m) and ±0.8 rad reaching 3 m + 0.15 s — against static bodies only (other cars and dynamic props are the corridor's and the recovery law's); a hit whose normal points more than 0.6 up is ground, not wall. Each side's closeness is the worst `(1 − hit/len)²`, and the steering gains `0.8 × (left − right)` away from the closer wall. A freshly armed reverse-and-turn escape turns toward the side the feelers see as open instead of alternating blind. Rear-end guard: a fellow opponent the authored `avoidOpponents=0` leaves unsensed is still looked for straight ahead (the corridor's ±2.4 m), out to the driver's stopping distance at the planned deceleration plus 20 m (capped at its look-ahead). The guard commits no pass — the flag keeps deciding whether the driver manoeuvres around the field — and only answers a closure: the demand `closing² / (2 × (gap − 5 m))` over the car's full braking lifts off from 0.15 and brakes at the demand from 0.3, and a standing car approached under 2 m/s is left to the escape and the feelers so the guard cannot pen the driver behind it. The human participant is not covered — `avoidPlayers=0` drivers still ram the player. Measured on the 16-event parked-Pro benchmark: hard (≥5 m/s) AI-on-AI impacts 203 → 112 while the total contact count stayed level, the hits replaced by low-speed nudges. | `mm2_app::racing_line` (`RouteCursor`, `CarLimits`, `plan_speed`, `pace`, `steer_toward`, `sense_walls`), `mm2_app::opponents` (`aim_distance`, `opponent_drive`) |
